WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.454 --> 00:00:06.370
MARTIN GANNHOLM: Good
afternoon, and thank you

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.620
for joining our session.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.860
My name is Martin Gannholm.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.470
And during the six years
that I've been at Google,

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.960
I've worked on and
led the teams that

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.990
have delivered a number
of the cloud products

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.000
you might be using, like
Compute Engine, Container

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.430
Engine, and Kubernetes.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.170
LOUIS RYAN: Hi, I'm Louis Ryan.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.240
I've been at Google
about nine years now.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.190
I've worked on
Google's API platforms.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.100
So when you make API calls
to Google, all of those

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.260
go through stuff that
my team works on.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.468
And also more recently, I've
been doing a lot of work

00:00:37.468 --> 00:00:38.010
on gRPC.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.880
But I'm going to hand
you back to Martin

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.770
who's going to show you
some interesting stuff.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.270
MARTIN GANNHOLM:
So what we're going

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.170
to talk to you about is based
on a lot of our experiences

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.370
building and running large-scale
cloud services at Google

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.930
and how we can bring this
kind of learning to Kubernetes

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.330
and you folks.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.780
So there's been a lot of
talk over the last few years

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.660
about zero ops and
server-less computing

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.570
and how these concepts allow
you to spend less of your time

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.840
managing and dealing
with infrastructure.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.460
In effect, they can let you
run ambitious apps at scale

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.900
without having to
know all the details.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.962
So if you use Google
App Engine or BigQuery,

00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:23.420
you're familiar
with how they allow

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.480
you to focus on your expertise
and writing your business logic

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.480
or your data processing, and
not managing all the machines

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.690
underneath the covers.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.494
So that's kind of the context.

00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:34.910
So we're going to talk to you
about some technologies that

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.260
are currently under
development in open source,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.190
and that will get you
on the way to being

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.545
able to operate
systems at scale,

00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:49.190
cheaply, efficiently,
and securely.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.690
So we consider these
building blocks

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:54.140
the beginning of a journey to
deliver the promise of zero ops

00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:00.410
and server-less computing in
the integrated hybrid cloud.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.070
So all the different parts
of your solution stacks,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.570
whether they're
running in-house,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.360
on-prem, or if they're in
a public or private cloud,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.930
whether you wrote the components
or whether you acquired them

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:17.470
from some third party, we will
cover all of those pieces.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.330
So there are three
intertwined principles

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.490
that we believe are
the foundation that

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.700
will get us there.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.240
The first is services,
not infrastructure.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.110
And what we mean
by that is we need

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.660
to look at it at a
service abstraction layer.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.700
And if we do that,
we can get away

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.610
from dealing with
the infrastructure.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.340
It's very common for
companies in recent years

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.320
to have a service
catalog that make

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.360
it easier for their
application developers

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.150
to sort of get bootstrapped
with different pieces.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.150
But oftentimes those are just
a piece of infrastructure,

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.630
like a virtual machine that gets
provisioned, some deployment

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.750
system or scripts run and
install things on them.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.100
And at that point, it's yours.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.470
It's very much like
getting a free puppy.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.780
And you quickly learn
that getting the puppy

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.790
was the easy part.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.040
You've now got to
take care of it.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.080
In contrast, often managed
capabilities behind an API

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.110
is the kind of thing
that is allowed

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:17.940
for the explosive adoption
of cloud services.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.550
And we like to
bring that same kind

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.220
of thinking to all the different
components that you build

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.110
or that you acquire
from other people.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:31.730
So choosing an abstraction
layer and getting it right

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.990
means a few things.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.320
You'll be able to separate the
construction, the operation,

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.730
and the usage of components
from each other, which gives you

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.250
more flexibility in how
you source the components

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.390
and how you manage them.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:50.720
You'll be able to inject key
behaviors in different control

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.540
points in this higher
level system, allowing

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.090
for many of the
intent-based functions that

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.040
will let you operate at scale
with little or no effort.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.570
You'll also be able to
compose your solutions

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.330
across a wide range of
infrastructures of environments

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.470
and technologies.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.800
And this really brings us
to the next principle--

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.390
interoperability.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:14.810
This is fundamentally about
choice and flexibility,

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.930
about giving you
a path from today

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.740
to tomorrow and to whatever
the future may bring.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:25.010
This includes legacy components
that you already have running

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.610
that you use, in-house developed
services that might be more

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:33.335
modern, third-party
software-as-a-service pieces.

00:04:33.335 --> 00:04:35.210
And all of these will
span multiple different

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:35.917
environments.

00:04:35.917 --> 00:04:38.000
And it's all about allowing
you to choose the best

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.540
option for each individual
piece without giving up

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.870
on the promise of zero
ops and server-less.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.000
And this is a vision that
is only realizable if we

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.570
succeed in the third pillar.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.210
We like automation at Google.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.790
We think that teaching
computers to execute

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:59.930
repetitive, error-prone
and large-scale tasks so

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.540
that humans can focus
on value-added pursuits

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.480
is a good thing.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.600
So for example, if you want to
apply an enterprise-wide policy

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.510
to all of your services,
that should be trivial.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.150
Do you want to
canary test an update

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.840
and then roll that out to
all of your zones or whatever

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.120
geographical spread
that you have

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.630
if no anomalies are detected
and do that automatically?

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.240
Yep, that's one of the
things you want to do.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.320
What if something wasn't right?

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:28.840
Automatic rollback.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.921
So the answer for us
is automate everything.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:40.110
So let's touch on two of the
hard problems to solve for.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.420
Let's just say it--
networking's hard.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.070
Doing networking
right is harder.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.230
And now you want
it to be secure?

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.700
You've got to be kidding.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.060
Nobody wants to deal
that, at least I don't.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.860
So we talk about
this as the space

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.550
between the micro-services.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.180
So the red lines here
represent that space.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.690
And so the questions are--

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.250
how do you securely
connect services together?

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:09.470
Can you do encrypted and
authenticated access?

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:13.690
Can you protect against DDoS or
even just unintentional packet

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.740
storms?

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:17.240
Credentials-- that
would be great

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.560
if you could flow credentials
through the system.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.070
But not just once, you
want key rotation, right?

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.140
You want these things to
update and manage that.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:25.860
So those are the
kinds of things that

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.860
would be better if you
didn't have to deal with.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.810
The second part is hybrid
cloud or multi-cloud.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.300
That can be hard.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.650
If you use a homogeneous
system, like Kubernetes,

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.314
for example, which
is multi-platform,

00:06:43.314 --> 00:06:44.980
you can run it in
many different places.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:49.970
And if you're all in on
that, everything's fine.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.430
But most people don't
have that luxury.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.060
So you have legacy.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.780
You have virtual machines.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.180
Those are the kinds
of things that you

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.254
have to sort of
include as you evolve.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.670
So some of the
questions you might

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.755
have is, well, if I'm
using a service on-prem,

00:07:06.755 --> 00:07:07.505
is that different?

00:07:07.505 --> 00:07:09.010
Do I have to
operate differently?

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:10.720
Do I have to deal
with it differently

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.710
than a third-party
acquired service?

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.810
How do I tell which
services I'm actually

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:21.070
dependent on across
my entire solution?

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:24.280
How do I enforce company
policies across those?

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.170
Or can only do it on
a subset that I have

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:28.180
under my immediate control?

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.090
And how do I get a handle on the
security aspect of this layout?

00:07:33.442 --> 00:07:34.900
So let's get more
concrete and talk

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.450
about the service abstraction.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.420
So we have partnered with
several other companies

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.250
around the Open
Service Broker API,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:48.040
which is a relatively new
standard in the making which

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.759
originally came
from Cloud Foundry.

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:51.550
So those of you familiar
with Cloud Foundry

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.220
will recognize these concepts.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.980
And so we're working
with these companies

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:05.950
on adopting and then extending
this great abstraction.

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.710
And so the importance
of a standard

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.160
is that services that are
written for consumption

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.500
in the Cloud Foundry
application will

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.760
be able to be used
unmodified from Kubernetes

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.110
with what we're going to show
you today and vice versa.

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:26.740
And so as people adapt their
services to this model,

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.500
the amount of places
you'll be able to use them

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.940
is going to be great.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:38.110
So let's pretend that I
am a service producer.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:41.289
And I want to package up
Google's cloud services

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:43.809
behind a broker
or inside a broker

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.810
so that people can
easily consume them.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.090
So we want to expose
our cloud platform APIs,

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.460
like you see them down
there, Cloud Storage, PubSub,

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.810
BigQuery.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.930
And so a broker can
have multiple services

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:00.900
that it's able to be a
factory for, essentially.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.020
So let's look at one
of these, Cloud SQL.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.080
So Cloud SQL has two
types of instances.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.910
If you look on the
Cloud console it

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:12.840
has a first gen and
a second generation.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.700
The Broker API has
a notion of a plan.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.180
Each service class can
have one or more plans.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.130
And so as a producer
I might choose

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:27.660
to use Gen 1 versus Gen 2 as the
two plans that I can offer you.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.550
So when you want to
get one of these,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:30.960
you choose which plan you want.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.140
And I can make that
bifurcation there.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:38.130
Alternately, I could choose
a different way to factor it.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.200
I could say, well,
what if there are

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.790
different fundamental sizes--

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:42.960
small, medium, large?

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.160
I could do that.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.380
That's another thing you
can do with the plan.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.960
Or a different type of
a way to look at it is--

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.570
what about standard versus
high availability deployments

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:52.900
of Cloud SQL?

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:55.435
So those are different choices
that as a service producer

00:09:55.435 --> 00:09:58.890
I have a lot of flexibility
with this API to specify.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.340
So when you go and create
a service instance--

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.830
so you have a service class
you want an instance of.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:07.860
You want to use it.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.950
So you create a
service instance.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:12.030
You also specify
which plan you want.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.250
And so here are some
screenshots from Cloud SQL.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:18.500
And you see on the left
are the options for Gen 1.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.570
And on the right there's
a long scrollable list

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.850
of options on Gen 2.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:28.999
And so the Service Broker
API allows for the difference

00:10:28.999 --> 00:10:30.040
between these two things.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:34.250
And in the future,
the parameters

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:37.010
that are valid for one
plan versus another

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:39.860
will be schematized and so
that we can then automate.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.980
We can actually have the user
interface parse through that

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:46.310
and present you the
options really nicely

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:48.440
and be able to show
you what options

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:52.328
are valid for which plan.

00:10:52.328 --> 00:10:55.560
So once you've created
a logical instance,

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.100
you want to use that
service from an application.

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:02.720
And rather than copying API
keys, IP addresses, configuring

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.580
SSL, and all those
kinds of things

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:10.310
that you today do in the in the
Cloud console, and then take

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:13.040
whatever information they give
you and apply those somehow

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.090
to your code, to your
config of your applications,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.700
the Broker API, in conjunction
with another piece,

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.880
the service controller, which
I'll get to in a second,

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.100
has a way of configuring
and passing this information

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:30.470
directly into your application,
saving you that step.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.270
So to illustrate this further,
the service controller

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:34.950
is a logical construct.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:37.290
And it's tied to your
runtime environment.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.170
So here you can see
the dotted box is meant

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:41.419
to be a Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:41.419 --> 00:11:43.710
And so inside of that there's
a service controller that

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.630
understands what it's
like, what it means

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.730
to be a Kubernetes application.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:52.620
So it sort of does some of
the work in this scenario.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.260
So the service catalog
has a few purposes.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.610
One, you can register brokers
with it, as many as you want.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:02.280
What it does is it asks
the brokers what are

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.900
the available service classes?

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.000
And then it sort of creates
an aggregated catalog

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.700
of those things.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:11.330
Additionally, the service
controller can let you--

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.130
and this functionality
doesn't exist today,

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.890
but this is one of
the things that we're

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.920
looking at is that this is
now a control point where

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.900
you can apply IAM policies.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:24.740
So that you can say,
well, certain people

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.661
can't access the user service.

00:12:26.661 --> 00:12:28.160
I can sort of lock
that down so they

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.200
can't bind to instances of
it, or they might not be

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:31.491
able to create instances of it.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.250
So once the user
decides which service

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:40.536
they want to use, for example
lets pick Cloud PubSub,

00:12:40.536 --> 00:12:41.910
they ask the
controller to create

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.160
an instance of the class.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:48.460
And the controller delegates
to the broker and says,

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.340
hey, why don't you
create this class--

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:51.960
create an instance from it.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.690
And then that, in turn, goes
and does whatever it does,

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:55.850
completely opaque.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.270
You have no idea what it does.

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:59.210
It could provision
a large amount

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:03.440
of virtual infrastructure, many
virtual machines or whatever.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.580
Or it could simply
just insert a row

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.007
in a database noting that you
now have a virtual instance.

00:13:09.007 --> 00:13:11.340
So it's a nice abstraction
because it allows the service

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.580
provider to do whatever
implementation they

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:18.120
want, and it's transparent.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:22.920
So, let's see, so now
we have the service,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.110
and now we want to bind
an application to it.

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.347
We want to use it
from an application.

00:13:27.347 --> 00:13:29.555
So here's an application,
just a Node.js application,

00:13:29.555 --> 00:13:31.810
not a Kubernetes cluster.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.310
And we want to tell
the system that we

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.230
want to bind these together.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:40.740
So we talk to the service
controller, create a binding.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.140
The service controller, once
again, delegates to the broker.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:48.300
The broker figures out
what are the coordinates,

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:52.410
where is the actual instance,
what IP address, which port,

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:53.724
those kinds of things.

00:13:53.724 --> 00:13:55.140
And again, it's
service-dependent,

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.920
but that's a very common type
of thing that it returns.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.650
And it also returns
credentials, credentials

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.070
that could be unique
for each binding

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.070
or could be shared
for all the bindings

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:09.480
to a particular instance.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.440
Again, this is a service--

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.530
it's part of the
service definition.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.020
So as a service producer,
you can choose what

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.300
makes sense in your scenario.

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:21.550
And the last step that the
service controller does

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:23.920
is that it sort of creates
the virtual binding

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.610
and injects those
credentials and those

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:31.180
coordinates into the application
so they can very easily consume

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:31.870
it.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.540
And I'll show this in the
demo in a few minutes.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:38.440
So, yeah, in Kubernetes this is
done through secrets and config

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.720
maps, and the application
just accesses them

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.410
through environment variables.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.730
So here are the pieces we're
going to talk to today.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.730
So these are three different
open source things.

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:50.620
I talked about the
service broker.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.630
I hinted at the service catalog.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:54.440
That's what I call the
