Can we show the
slides on the screen?

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:48.990
So what are some of
the key takeaways?

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:51.620
This is the app
that we modified,

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:54.320
and we showed how we
can easily connect

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:57.350
to preexisting services
from a new app and also

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.240
how to provision and
connect to a cloud

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:01.114
service in the same way.

00:27:01.114 --> 00:27:03.530
One of the existing services,
the payment service here, we

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.250
didn't actually deal with that.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:07.100
But what's interesting
is that it's actually

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:09.080
exposed as a service as well.

00:27:09.080 --> 00:27:11.900
And so there's a way that
you can wrap existing legacy

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:13.610
things as services.

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:15.560
And those of you familiar
with Cloud Foundry

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:18.620
will recognize that
same capability.

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:20.440
And so a lot of the
benefits that you're

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:23.420
going to see, especially in
Louie's part of the talk,

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:26.850
are then applicable to
something like a legacy service,

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:28.910
like payments as well,
because it now gets

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:31.890
wrapped in this abstraction.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:34.560
We also showed how we
need to know knowledge

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:37.110
of where the service
came from or how

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:39.014
to negotiate credentials.

00:27:39.014 --> 00:27:40.680
The necessary information
just showed up

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:44.430
in secrets and then
environment variables.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.920
Now let me hand off to
Louis, who will show you

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:48.490
some even cooler stuff.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:57.510
LOUIS RYAN: Thank you, Martin.

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:03.442
Martin talked to
you a lot about how

00:28:03.442 --> 00:28:05.400
people are going to
acquire services, how we're

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.983
going to make sure that services
have well-known relationships

00:28:07.983 --> 00:28:09.690
with each other.

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:11.940
And now I'm going to show
you some of the things

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.650
that we'd like to
do underneath all

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:17.020
of that to help make
that work better

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:19.560
and to help you get some insight
into how those services are

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:23.340
talking to each other and be
able to start imposing policies

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.160
on the conversations that
those services are having

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:28.359
with each other.

00:28:28.359 --> 00:28:30.150
And there's a term
floating around nowadays

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:32.316
that I think is gaining
some momentum for all of us,

00:28:32.316 --> 00:28:34.140
and it's the service mesh.

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:35.660
You have lots of these services.

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:37.200
They're all talking
to each other.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:40.170
It's not a network in the
traditional packet sense.

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:43.410
It's a network of services
and their relationships

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:46.810
with each other and all the
things that they want to do.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:51.280
So I think of a service mesh
as a network for services.

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:54.810
So let's talk about
that a little bit.

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.066
I got the wrong button.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:04.050
So what would you want a network
of services to do for you?

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:06.802
Well, when we build services
today, we want reliability.

00:29:06.802 --> 00:29:08.010
We want them to perform well.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:14.400
We want them to be able to deal
with transient failure modes.

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:16.080
Maybe if I bring up
a service and it's

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:19.320
talking to some other service,
and that service has a failure

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:22.140
mode, maybe I can't reach it
or maybe or one of the jobs

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:22.830
goes down.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.690
I'd like the network,
the service mesh,

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.406
to take care of that for me.

00:29:27.406 --> 00:29:31.779
We'd also like the service mesh
to help us with performance.

00:29:31.779 --> 00:29:33.570
When I bring up lots
of jobs, and maybe I'm

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:35.550
bringing up stateful
jobs and stateless jobs

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:39.480
and doing all these types
of things in production,

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:42.120
routing traffic to a stateful
job when it hasn't had time

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.310
to warm up its cache is
probably a really bad thing

00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:46.255
to do for latency.

00:29:46.255 --> 00:29:47.880
And it might even be
a really bad thing

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:50.730
to do for availability
if cache loading is

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:52.440
a very expensive
operation, and you

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:56.460
start to get these
cascading failure modes.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.430
We also want the network,
the service mesh,

00:29:58.430 --> 00:30:00.080
to help us with visibility.

00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:03.800
I'd like to know which services
are talking to each other.

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:07.120
I'd like to know if there
are services in my deployment

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:09.512
that I don't expect to be there.

00:30:09.512 --> 00:30:11.720
So we'd like to have a lot
more visibility about what

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:14.930
the behavior is in this network.

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:18.680
And I'd like to
have control, right?

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.020
When a service is talking
to another service,

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:24.170
it would be very nice if I could
go and interpose in that flow

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:26.790
and say, no, you're not allowed
to make this communication.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:28.760
You're violating some
corporate policy,

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:30.690
or you're using too
much of a resource

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:33.330
or doing these types of things.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:38.270
So how do we make
all of that happen?

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.140
Can we switch back to the demo?

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:42.700
So meanwhile in ops land--

00:30:49.990 --> 00:30:52.446
so while Martin was doing
all these wonderful things,

00:30:52.446 --> 00:30:54.570
one of the things we also
spun up on the background

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:56.414
is some monitoring.

00:30:56.414 --> 00:30:58.330
So all the services are
talking to each other.

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:00.197
They're generating traffic.

00:31:00.197 --> 00:31:00.780
Wait a minute.

00:31:03.402 --> 00:31:04.770
Did we switch to the demo?

00:31:08.039 --> 00:31:09.440
Thank you.

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:11.037
So here's a typical
Grafana dashboard.

00:31:11.037 --> 00:31:13.120
It's showing you the traffic
between the services.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:16.480
We're showing you a simple
request-per-second metric.

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:18.430
And you can see that
we've annotated the graph

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:20.260
with the services, right?

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:23.040
So you know that
the bookfe service

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:25.630
is talking to purchasers
is talking to users.

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:27.400
And for some really
strange reason,

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:31.990
it's doing 60 QPS
of traffic, Martin?

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:35.150
This is the apps' guys running
load test in production again.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:43.270
Well, let's go stop
that, because that's just

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:45.250
bad practice.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:51.660
And that looked like it
was the books front-end.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:55.240
Let's see-- books front-end
talking to the user service.

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:58.960
And Martin definitely did
not buy 50 books a second.

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:02.336
So let's go deal with that.

00:32:02.336 --> 00:32:06.060
So we go back to our instances.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.311
And we can go look
at the user service.

00:32:09.311 --> 00:32:11.310
And we see we have our
binding-- books front-end

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.860
talking to the user service.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.870
So let's dig in there.

00:32:16.870 --> 00:32:19.870
And notice this link,
adds network function.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:20.820
So this is very rough.

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:24.900
This is very much demo alpha
quality stuff at this point.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.577
But if I go in here and
say add network function,

00:32:28.577 --> 00:32:30.660
I can start to take some
action on the interaction

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:32.640
between those two services.

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:36.720
And in this case, I'm
going to impose a quota.

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:38.580
And I don't think Martin
really wants to buy

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:40.490
more than five bucks a second--

00:32:40.490 --> 00:32:41.370
five books a second.

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:43.800
So I'm going to turn that on.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:46.590
Now, what happened
under the hood,

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:48.930
the system is aware
of this binding.

00:32:48.930 --> 00:32:51.862
It's going to take that
notion of relationship,

00:32:51.862 --> 00:32:53.820
and it's going to push
a piece of configuration

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:57.120
down into a system and
say, hey, for this binding,

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:01.140
this relationship between two
surfaces, we want this quota.

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:03.330
So let's hop back on over
to the Grafana dashboard

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:07.060
and see if anything happened.

00:33:07.060 --> 00:33:09.302
And look at that.

00:33:09.302 --> 00:33:11.260
We see the error rate
start to spike up, right?

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:13.830
The book's front-end
services, or this load test

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:16.280
that Martin is running,
is still sending traffic.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:18.080
But now we're rejecting it.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:19.270
It's getting dropped.

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:21.686
And actually, it's not even
going into the service itself.

00:33:21.686 --> 00:33:24.295
It's being dropped
earlier in the network.

00:33:24.295 --> 00:33:26.170
And so we're seeing the
request will actually

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:28.720
start to cap out at 5
QPS, the valid ones.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:31.640
And all the other traffic
is going to get rejected.

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:33.521
There are lots of
these types of things

00:33:33.521 --> 00:33:35.020
that we can do in
this network if we

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:36.644
had this amount of
information if we're

00:33:36.644 --> 00:33:40.180
able to interpose in the
traffic in sensible ways.

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:44.182
So how do we do
this type of thing?

00:33:44.182 --> 00:33:46.057
And can we switch back
to the slides, please?

00:33:55.797 --> 00:33:57.230
Although, Grafana's very pretty.

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:59.646
We could look at it for another
few minutes if you'd like.

00:34:07.722 --> 00:34:10.914
Can we go back to the slides?

00:34:10.914 --> 00:34:13.030
Thank you.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:15.864
So how do we do this?

00:34:15.864 --> 00:34:17.530
Well, the first thing
that we need to do

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:18.996
is we need to capture traffic.

00:34:18.996 --> 00:34:20.620
We can't really do
anything if we don't

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:22.870
know what's moving around.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.239
So here's a little subset
of the application.

00:34:25.239 --> 00:34:28.480
We have the books app,
and we have users service.

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:32.590
And the books app is calling
the user service via its API.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:37.190
Well, one of the things that
we did before Martin started

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.719
driving the demo is we installed
a component into the Kubernetes

00:34:40.719 --> 00:34:41.290
cluster.

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:43.270
This is part of the
Istio project, which

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:44.884
Martin alluded to earlier.

00:34:44.884 --> 00:34:46.300
And it's called
the proxy manager.

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:47.630
And what the proxy
manager does is

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:49.005
it listens to the
Kubernetes API.

00:34:49.005 --> 00:34:53.710
And when you spin up pods, it
rewrites the pods on the fly

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:55.120
and injects proxies into them.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:01.640
And then it also rewrites
the networking rules

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:03.940
inside Kubernetes.

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:06.300
So instead of that
traffic going directly

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.330
from the books front-end
to the users service,

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:10.040
it's actually going
through proxies.

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:11.873
And it's going through
a proxy on each side.

00:35:11.873 --> 00:35:13.650
And we'll talk a
little bit about why

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:16.100
we do those types of things.

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:17.920
So now that we're
capturing traffic,

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:19.836
we can start to do
interesting things with it.

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:25.017
We could start to inject
network functions.

00:35:27.602 --> 00:35:29.560
There's different classes
of network functions.

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:32.685
But we'll talk a little bit
about some of them here.

00:35:32.685 --> 00:35:34.810
Maybe we want to roll out
a new version of the user

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:37.919
service, user service v2.

00:35:37.919 --> 00:35:40.210
If you don't want to send
all of the production traffic

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:41.354
to it live, right?

00:35:41.354 --> 00:35:43.270
Maybe you just want to
send 1% of the traffic.

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:45.700
So you want to qualify
that service in production

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:49.420
and make sure that it's stable
before you cut everything over.

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:53.240
The proxy that was injected
into the books at V

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:56.271
can now start to split the
traffic on your behalf.

00:35:56.271 --> 00:35:58.020
And you can do this
at the operator level.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:59.830
You don't have to go and
change the application code

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:00.370
to do this.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:01.960
This is a very important thing.

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:11.480
Maybe the user
service is unreliable.

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:13.980
And so instead of the books app
having to deal with the fact

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:16.760
that its calls to the book
user service are flaky,

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:19.080
the proxy can actually
do the retries on behalf

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:21.150
of the service itself.

00:36:21.150 --> 00:36:23.130
And so as long as the
proxy has some awareness

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:25.296
of what type of operation
it is-- is it item potent?

00:36:25.296 --> 00:36:27.690
Is it safe to retry?

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:29.980
It can actually do these
things on your behalf.

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:33.945
And so if you have
a system where

00:36:33.945 --> 00:36:35.820
you have this kind of
intermittent flakiness,

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:37.903
but it's not due to anything
that you can directly

00:36:37.903 --> 00:36:39.930
control right now,
as an operator,

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:42.610
it's very often the
best of bad choices

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:47.220
is to start turning on retries
of exponential back-off.

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:50.370
Maybe you want to move the
service up into the cloud.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:52.622
Maybe the user service is
this vast, enormous thing,

00:36:52.622 --> 00:36:54.705
with hundreds of nodes in
seven different regions.

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:01.030
And now you want the proxy to
do smart intelligent things

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:03.129
on your behalf, like
load balancing, right?

00:37:03.129 --> 00:37:05.545
Maybe that load balancing is
going to be regionally aware.

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:12.570
Maybe you might want
that load balancing

00:37:12.570 --> 00:37:16.400
to do something like
optimize for latency, right?

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:18.870
So you're going to send
all of your traffic

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:22.590
to the most local node possible
until it starts to fill up,

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:25.167
and then you'll start to shard
it over to some other region.

00:37:25.167 --> 00:37:27.000
Maybe you want to
maximize for availability,

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:28.980
and you just kind of keep an
eye on the back-end health

00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:31.751
of each service, and the proxy
can be aware of these things.

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:37.220
Now, it's worth
noting that we didn't

00:37:37.220 --> 00:37:39.680
have to rewrite any
application code to do this.

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:41.780
As Martin mentioned
earlier, the books front-end

00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:42.980
was written in Node.js.

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:45.980
And user service, maybe
it's written in Go.

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:49.040
And the purchasing service is
written in .NET and all these

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:49.540
things.

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:52.910
So you have this big,
complex, polyglot environment.

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:55.510
Implementing sophisticated
network functions like these

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:56.376
is pretty expensive.

00:37:56.376 --> 00:37:58.250
It actually takes a lot
of engineering effort

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:00.720
to get it right and
to do it consistently.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:03.800
And consistency is what
really matters here, right?

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:06.500
If one piece of this network
doesn't really play well,

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:08.250
you can start to have
nasty things happen.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:11.290
You can have cascading
failure modes.

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:13.444
And so having the proxy do
this work on your behalf

00:38:13.444 --> 00:38:15.110
is actually massively
beneficial, right?

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:17.193
As long as it is consistent
and it does them well,

00:38:17.193 --> 00:38:19.790
you don't have to worry about
the application developers

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:22.190
consuming some fancy
network run time,

00:38:22.190 --> 00:38:24.080
trying to write
exponential back-off code

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:25.520
for the 15th time.

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:29.170
You just don't want to
be doing those things.

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.620
So what else can we do?

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:33.850
Well, we can inject identity.

00:38:33.850 --> 00:38:36.690
So another piece that is still
installed into the Kubernetes

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:40.380
cluster is a
certificate authority.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:42.930
And when we bring
the proxies up,

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:44.535
we also inject
credentials into them.

00:38:44.535 --> 00:38:46.410
Those credentials, in
the case of Kubernetes,

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:49.680
are tied to Kubernetes
service accounts.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:51.950
Now the proxies can
use those credentials,

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:55.780
those certificates, to enable
crypto between the proxies.

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:58.970
So we can do mutual TLS.

00:38:58.970 --> 00:39:03.580
So now both applications,
both nodes of this graph,

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:05.660
can strongly verify
who's calling them.

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:06.630
They know it precisely.

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:07.950
And it's not just
some random piece

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:09.199
of code running in my cluster.

00:39:09.199 --> 00:39:11.470
No, it's something running
with that authority.

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:16.840
Even if the user's
application isn't

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:18.820
written using some
standard protocol,

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:20.500
maybe it's not using HTP, right?

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:23.180
Maybe it's a database with
its own proprietary binary

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:24.497
protocol.

00:39:24.497 --> 00:39:26.080
So maybe you can't
tunnel the identity

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:28.371
all the way down into it,
because it's protocol doesn't

00:39:28.371 --> 00:39:31.385
matter or it won't
let you do that.

00:39:31.385 --> 00:39:33.760
But at least you know the
traffic between those two nodes

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:37.340
is secure and is tied
to that identity.

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:38.810
And if it's tied
to that identity,

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.760
we can start to do
some things with it.

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:44.660
We can start injecting
policy associated with it.

00:39:44.660 --> 00:39:47.180
Maybe the books application is
owned by Martin's development

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:48.080
group.

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:51.710
And maybe the user service
is owned by my group.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:54.449
And I think his development
team is a bunch of cowboys,

00:39:54.449 --> 00:39:56.240
and I'm just going to
shut them off, right?

00:39:56.240 --> 00:39:59.650
I can do those types of things.

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:03.220
There's lots of interesting
things we can do with policy.

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:06.120
So another component that gets
injected into the Kubernetes

00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:09.320
environment is this
thing we call the mixer.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.120
And it can do a wide
range of things.

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:14.900
So I showed you earlier--

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:16.460
we showed you monitoring, right?

00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:18.420
We inject this component,
and the proxy's

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.300
sending all this
information to it

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:22.357
about the behavior
of the network.

00:40:22.357 --> 00:40:24.190
And we simply flow that
into Grafana, right?

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:25.981
We flow it through
Prometheas into Grafana,

00:40:25.981 --> 00:40:29.220
and we had some nice dashboards
and things like that.

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:31.922
And I can visualize my
network and the behavior

00:40:31.922 --> 00:40:33.130
of the services between them.

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:34.963
And we could have done
that with Stackdriver

00:40:34.963 --> 00:40:38.120
or some other monitoring tool.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:39.450
We also did quota, right?

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:40.950
And in the example
that I showed you

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:44.340
we have a quota component
running inside this.

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:47.690
And on the user side
it said, hey, I'm

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:49.400
supposed to enforce
5 DPS of quota,

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:51.965
and it just prevented the calls
on that server-side proxy.

00:40:51.965 --> 00:40:54.980
It prevented them from actually
getting into the user service.

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:56.690
And if there was
mutual trust, then we

00:40:56.690 --> 00:40:58.430
could have actually done
that check all the way back

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:00.440
up at the book side, and
the traffic would never

00:41:00.440 --> 00:41:02.930
even hit the physical network.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:06.020
But in worlds where you span
multiple trust boundaries,

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:07.760
you really do need
to make sure that you

00:41:07.760 --> 00:41:10.880
enforce ACL-like checks
in the same trust

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:13.690
domain as the service.

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:19.820
We could also have done
authentication in ACL-ing.

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:22.214
So maybe you have multiple
authentication schemes

00:41:22.214 --> 00:41:22.880
in your network.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:24.920
Maybe you're using the
OAuth for [? signjots ?]

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:27.410
or who knows what.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:30.230
And those credentials
are flowing through,

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:32.240
let's say, HTP calls.

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:34.280
You can delegate them
out to the service,

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:35.660
and they can make sure
that they're consistently

00:41:35.660 --> 00:41:36.826
enforced across the network.

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:42.430
And also you can enforce
ACL policies, right?

00:41:42.430 --> 00:41:43.846
If I have that
notion of identity,

00:41:43.846 --> 00:41:46.530
whether it's a
mutual TLS identity,

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:48.530
maybe it's the Kubernetes
service account, maybe

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:51.399
it's something else, it
doesn't really matter.

00:41:51.399 --> 00:41:53.190
You can use that to
perform ACL-ing checks.

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:56.870
And a lot of companies out
there have standard ACL policy

00:41:56.870 --> 00:41:57.970
systems.

00:41:57.970 --> 00:42:00.530
And you can use this
to integrate that.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:02.930
And this is probably
the most important point

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:05.186
is that the system
is easy to customize.

00:42:05.186 --> 00:42:07.310
And so what we've really
done is set up a framework

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:09.393
where we provide a whole
bunch of standard signals

00:42:09.393 --> 00:42:12.759
about the traffic
flowing between services.

00:42:12.759 --> 00:42:15.050
And then we provide this
convenient runtime integration

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:21.059
point, where you can go and
put control over that traffic.

00:42:21.059 --> 00:42:22.850
So it will know which
service it came from,

00:42:22.850 --> 00:42:26.510
which service it's going
to, what the operation is,

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:29.130
what region it came from, what
credential or identity does

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:32.660
that have, how big is the thing.

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:34.856
You can enforce whatever
policy you want.

00:42:34.856 --> 00:42:36.980
And so this mixer thing,
the current implementation

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:37.730
is written in Go.

00:42:37.730 --> 00:42:40.070
We think Go is a good language
for people to write extensions

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:41.720
in and to plug them
into this framework

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:43.400
and then just deploy
into the cluster.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:54.340
So in conclusion, we
have our principles.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:57.240
And Martin talked a lot
about these in the beginning.

00:42:57.240 --> 00:43:00.520
And the big one being services
not infrastructure, right?

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:03.940
What we're trying to focus
on is services as units

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:08.050
that you can find,
acquire, consume,

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:10.440
and then operate, right?

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:15.100
And not the lower-level building
blocks necessary to do all

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:16.510
those types of things--

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:20.460
bringing the level
of extraction up.

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:21.860
We talked about
interoperability.

00:43:21.860 --> 00:43:25.090
And in particular with the
open service broker API--

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:26.890
it's a community effort.

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:29.970
And we showed you a
way to bring services

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:34.560
from other environments
into your workplace, right?

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:35.760
I had my own catalog.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:37.050
I could take Google's catalog.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:38.970
I could take some
other vendor's catalog,

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:41.730
bring them in, and start
to work with those things.

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:43.890
Or maybe as an
internal thing, right,

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:47.547
I just create my own
catalog and I curate it.

00:43:47.547 --> 00:43:50.130
And that's all that my internal
developers are allowed to use.

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:51.713
And there's this
qualification process

00:43:51.713 --> 00:43:54.420
to get things into the catalog.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:57.000
And then we talked
about automation.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:00.030
We want to make sure that
everything is automatable.

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:02.130
So we walked you through
our UI, and we showed you

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:06.070
a bunch of command line
stuff, and I did some things

00:44:06.070 --> 00:44:08.370
to enforce policy.

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:11.350
And then I showed you
some of how that worked.

00:44:11.350 --> 00:44:14.700
But everything here
is driven by an API.

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:18.000
So if you want to change the
policies around how services

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:21.691
talk to each other, you make
API calls to do that, right?

00:44:21.691 --> 00:44:23.940
You probably need to have a
lot of privilege to do it,

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:26.480
but that's what you
should be doing.

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:29.437
And so you can start to automate
the rollout of services,

00:44:29.437 --> 00:44:31.770
automate the acquisition of
services from third parties,

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:33.145
and do all these
types of things.

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:38.250
This is entirely open.

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:40.810
This is a collaborative effort.

00:44:40.810 --> 00:44:44.850
Google is not doing this on
its own or anything like it.

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:47.810
And so Martin talked about
the open service broker.

00:44:47.810 --> 00:44:50.920
He also talked about the
Kubernetes service catalog.

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:53.800
And I talked a little bit about
the service mesh and Istio.

00:44:53.800 --> 00:44:54.850
And these are the
companies that we're

00:44:54.850 --> 00:44:57.183
working with on a daily basis
to make that stuff happen.

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:07.340
So here's some
useful links, right?

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:10.640
You can go and follow these
links, read about this stuff.

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:13.130
As Martin warned you
at the beginning,

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:17.750
things are alpha, sharp,
pointy, dangerous,

00:45:17.750 --> 00:45:19.222
borderline unreadable.

00:45:19.222 --> 00:45:21.680
Maybe you have to read the code
to figure out how it works.

00:45:21.680 --> 00:45:24.785
It could be quite painful.

00:45:24.785 --> 00:45:26.660
So hopefully the next
time we give this talk,

00:45:26.660 --> 00:45:29.118
there'll be nice shiny docs
when you go follow these links,

00:45:29.118 --> 00:45:31.220
and everything will be
entirely understandable.

00:45:31.220 --> 00:45:33.020
And you'd all be using
it, and you wouldn't

00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:34.520
need to ask me any questions.

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:38.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

